`distict-prime-divisors-of.ts`: In my edit, after `expand(factorAsBig === 1n || factor === Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY ? 1n : factorAsBig)` came `...expand(coFactorAsBig)` — wait, in the new version, factorAsBig is already 1n when leftover is 1n. When leftover > 1 but the rho fails (∞): factorAsBig = leftover → expand(leftover) → infinite recursion!! If pollardRho returns ∞ (complete failure), it becomes expand(leftover) → same input → stack overflow. Guard: if factor === ∞ and leftover is not prime: treat as prime (a number that's probably prime but failed the check) — better yet: fallback `factorAsBig = leftover` (treat as prime leaf). That is, on failure: return [Number is unsafe] — as a safeguard: if factor is ∞: assume leftover is prime and push it:
```
const factorIsGenuine = factor !== Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
const factorAsBig = factorIsGenuine ? BigInt(factor) : leftover;
const coFactorAsBig = factorIsGenuine ? leftover / BigInt(factor) : 1n;
```
On the off chance that rho completely fails, we'd end up listing a composite as a "prime" — the test's golden (F_25 = 75025 = 5²·3001: 3001 is prime) should continue to pass. Acceptable as a documented degradation. Let me check the content I wrote earlier: I wrote it like this:
```
