content.thinking:
The current `expand` is: `return leftover === 1n ? [] : [Number(factorAsBig), ...expand(coFactorAsBig)]`. When leftover > 1 and rho succeeds: factorAsBig = factor, coFactor = leftover/factor: returns [factor, ...expand(coFactor)] — recursive on the smaller part ✓ terminates. When leftover is prime: factorAsBig = leftover, coFactor = 1n: [leftover, ...expand(1n)] = [leftover, ...[]] ✓. When ∞: factorAsBig = leftover, coFactor = 1n: [leftover] (degrades to treating as prime) ✓ no infinite recursion.

Hmm, wait — there's a subtle bug: when leftover is prime, `Number(factorAsBig)`: Number(leftover) — if leftover is prime but > 2^53 (a 40-digit prime from F_200!), Number() loses precision → distinctPrimeDivisorsOf returns a number that's garbage as a bigint-sized prime!! The factor list is typed as number[] — it can't hold 40-digit primes. For bands up to n ≤ 200, F_200 ≈ 2.8e41: could have 41-digit prime factors > 2^53. In the band density runs (n ≤ 100: F_100 ≈ 3.5e20 < 2^68: can exceed 2^53 (9e15)! For example, a 21-digit prime factor: 1e20 > 9e15 ✓ possible).

Hmm. But: in the band context, all we need from the factor list is: (a) 1/p for the reciprocal sum (needs a precision of about p < 1e21 or so: 64-bit double precision for a 21-digit number: relative 1e-16: is that enough for B[n] to 6 digits? B[n] ~ 1/p: relative precision 1e-16 → no problem), (b) the order computation fibModulo(n, p) needs an exact p as a bigint. The current design: distinctPrimeDivisorsOf returns number[] → precision is lost → the order computation silently uses the wrong prime → silently produces wrong results!! This is exactly the class of silent type bug the owner wants to eliminate.

